WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.930
In the last module, we took
first steps towards understanding

00:00:02.930 --> 00:00:06.470
the electrical properties
of individual neurons.

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:09.980
We learned how electrical
forces and diffusion give rise

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:11.780
to membrane potentials,

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:15.270
and we learned how cells can generate
and propagate signals called action

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:19.670
potentials, or 'spikes',
along the membrane.

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:23.280
Understanding the properties of
the neuronal membrane is essential,

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:27.570
but understanding just these properties
isn't sufficient to give us insight

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:33.620
into collective behavior of the billions
of connected neurons in our brains.

00:00:33.620 --> 00:00:37.820
Luckily for us, we can approach
neuroscience at many different scales

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:41.700
and levels of analysis, and we don't
have to confront the full complexity

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:43.550
of everything all at once.

00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:47.710
That's what we'll be exploring
throughout the rest of this course

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:51.720
as we slowly go from our understanding
of single molecules such as ion

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:52.220
channels'

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:55.730
...to the electrical behavior of neurons

00:00:55.730 --> 00:00:59.690
...to their collective
behavior in small circuits

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:03.240
...and finally onto how they become
organized in large functional regions

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:07.600
of the brain.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:09.740
Let's start simple, though.

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:13.730
Since we've examined one neuron, a
logical next question is how do two

00:01:13.730 --> 00:01:16.620
neurons connect with one another?

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:21.940
We'll first examine some basic
cellular anatomy of neurons.

00:01:21.940 --> 00:01:25.340
So far, we haven't made too much of
the fact that the majority of neurons

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:26.160
are 'polarized'

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:27.040
cells.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:30.050
That is, they have one
portion of the cell

00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:33.640
for receiving inputs and another
portion for sending outputs.

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:39.420
The parts of the cells that are
specialized for receiving inputs from

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:41.650
other cells are called 'dendrites.'

00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:48.080
The word 'dendrite' comes from the
Greek word 'dendron', meaning tree,

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:52.020
and as you can see the dendrites
have a branching, tree-like shape.

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:55.910
A signal received by a dendrite
is passed to the cell body.

00:01:55.910 --> 00:02:00.470
If there is a sufficient depolarization
of the cell body membrane

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:05.660
to initiate an action potential, then an
action potential is sent down the axon.

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:13.830
The axon then carries the propagating
action potential to another neuron.

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:17.000
So what actually happens at the
boundary between two neurons,

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:22.052
between the axon of one neuron
and the dendrite of another?

00:02:22.052 --> 00:02:23.760
This interface is
called a 'synapse',

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:25.451
and it'll be the focus of this lesson.

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:33.030
There are two general types of synapses
that we'll cover in depth later:

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:35.630
electrical synapses,
and chemical synapses.

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:41.440
Electrical synapses are less
common in our own nervous systems,

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:47.120
but they're simpler to think
about, so let's start with them.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:50.490
Electrical synapses are
basically pores between two cell

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:53.240
that allow ions to pass through.

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:55.590
They allow the passage
of that electrical signal

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:58.620
through to a neighboring
cell without much fuss.

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:02.680
It's not so different than just
combining two cells into one larger

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:04.230
cell.

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:07.890
There are lots of reasons that nature
might need synapses like this from time

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:11.550
to time -- they're fast, and they
allow cells to couple together with

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:13.430
a high degree of synchronicity.

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:18.660
But most neurons are connected together
by a much more complicated structure

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:19.920
called a chemical synapse.

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:25.310
In a chemical synapse, rather than
simply passing along an electrical

00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:29.390
signal from one cell to another, the
action potential travels to the end

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:33.370
of the axon and causes a chemical to
be released into a very small space

00:03:33.370 --> 00:03:36.610
between the two neurons called
the 'synaptic cleft'.

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:42.900
This chemical is taken up
by the downstream neuron,

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:44.650
on the other side of the cleft.

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:47.680
This chemical signal can
cause the downstream neuron

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:50.760
to depolarize its membrane,
converting the chemical signal back

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:57.620
into an electrical one, or it can
have other effects on the cell.

00:03:57.620 --> 00:03:59.940
This chemical step is
slower than transmission

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:02.270
across an electrical
synapse, but it opens up

00:04:02.270 --> 00:04:05.360
an enormously diverse repertoire
of different and more complex

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:08.410
kinds of signaling,
and synaptic function

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:11.610
plays a critical role in
computations performed by neurons.

00:04:11.610 --> 00:04:16.779
We'll spend the the rest of this
unit exploring the inner workings

00:04:16.779 --> 00:04:21.100
of chemical synapses, on our way to
beginning to look at how networks

00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:24.830
of interconnected neurons
give rise to behavior.

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:28.930
We'll also look at the role
of defective synaptic physiology

00:04:28.930 --> 00:04:31.460
in neurological and
psychiatric disorders,

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:35.520
and we'll see how synapses can be
targeted by various psychoactive drugs

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:36.270
and poisons.

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:42.640
Finally we'll wrap up by looking
at how synapse can change with time,

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:46.970
in response to external stimuli, playing
a foundational role in how we learn

00:04:46.970 --> 00:04:48.700
and remember.

